Use the `system` property directly in the options object:

```ts
import { generateText } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

const { text } = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-4o'),
  system: 'You are a helpful assistant that only answers in haiku.',
  prompt: 'What is the sky?',
});
```

`system` accepts a `string` or `string[]` (useful for concatenating multiple system instructions).

You *can* also put a `{ role: 'system', content: '...' }` message inside the `messages` array, but the dedicated `system` property is the idiomatic approach.

## Why

In AI SDK v3, the `messages` parameter is typed as `CoreMessage[]`, which only allows `role: 'user'` or `role: 'assistant'`. The `system` role was removed from the messages array and moved to a dedicated `system` parameter.

## Fix

Extract any system messages from your stored array and pass them via the `system` option:

```ts
import { generateText } from 'ai';

// Your messages loaded from the DB (old format)
const storedMessages = [
  { role: 'system', content: 'You are a helpful assistant.' },
  { role: 'user', content: 'Hello' },
  { role: 'assistant', content: 'Hi there!' },
  { role: 'user', content: 'How are you?' },
];

// Separate system from the rest
const systemMessages = storedMessages.filter(m => m.role === 'system');
const messages = storedMessages.filter(m => m.role !== 'system');

const result = await generateText({
  model: 'gpt-4o',
  system: systemMessages.map(m => m.content).join('\n'),
  messages,
});
```

If you only ever have one system message, you can simplify:

```ts
const system = storedMessages.find(m => m.role === 'system')?.content ?? '';
const messages = storedMessages.filter(m => m.role !== 'system');

await generateText({ model: 'gpt-4o', system, messages });
```

## Going forward

When persisting conversations, store the system prompt separately (e.g., a `system_prompt` column or a config table) rather than mixing it into the message history. This keeps your stored `messages` array conformant to the SDK's `CoreMessage[]` type.

Use the `maxSteps` option (or `stopWhen: stepCountIs(n)`):

```ts
import { generateText, stepCountIs } from "ai";
import { openai } from "@ai-sdk/openai";

const result = await generateText({
  model: openai("gpt-4o"),
  prompt: "Plan a trip to Tokyo",
  tools: { search, bookFlight, bookHotel },
  maxSteps: 5, // stops after 5 tool-calling steps
});
```

Or equivalently with `stopWhen`:

```ts
stopWhen: stepCountIs(5),
```

`maxSteps` is just shorthand for `stopWhen: stepCountIs(n)`. Both work with `generateText` and `streamText`.

## `Agent` class

Use the **`Agent`** class from the `ai` package. It defaults to **`maxSteps: 1`**, meaning it makes a single model call (and executes any returned tool calls) without looping back to the model for a follow-up turn.

```ts
import { Agent } from "ai";
import { openai } from "@ai-sdk/openai";

const agent = new Agent({
  model: openai("gpt-4o"),
  tools: {
    getWeather: {
      description: "Get the current weather",
      parameters: z.object({ city: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ city }) => `Sunny, 72°F in ${city}`,
    },
  },
  // maxSteps: 1  ← default
});

const result = await agent.generate("What's the weather in Paris?");
```

If you want the agent to loop (model → tools → model → tools → …), raise it:

```ts
const agent = new Agent({
  model: openai("gpt-4o"),
  tools: { /* … */ },
  maxSteps: 5, // up to 5 model calls in the loop
});
```

> **Tip:** If you're using the streaming API (`agent.stream()`), the same `maxSteps` applies.

Use `generateObject` from the `ai` package:

```ts
import { generateObject } from "ai";
import { openai } from "@ai-sdk/openai";
import { z } from "zod";

const schema = z.object({
  name: z.string(),
  age: z.number(),
  hobbies: z.array(z.string()),
});

const { object } = await generateObject({
  model: openai("gpt-4o"),
  schema,
  prompt: "Describe a fictional person named Alice, 30 years old, who likes hiking and painting.",
});

// `object` is typed as { name: string; age: number; hobbies: string[] }
console.log(object.name); // "Alice"
```

`object` is already parsed and validated against your Zod schema — no manual `JSON.parse` or `schema.parse()` needed.

**Key points:**

- The return type of `object` is inferred from your schema via `z.infer<typeof schema>`.
- If the model's output doesn't match the schema, it throws a `NoObjectGeneratedError` (or similar) rather than returning invalid data.
- You can also use `maxTokens`, `temperature`, `system`, etc. as options.

For streaming, use `streamObject` instead — it returns a `ReadableStream` and a `fullStream` you can consume incrementally.

# Streaming a Structured Object

Use `streamObject` on the server and `useObject` on the client. The `partialObject` value updates as each field is parsed, letting you render incrementally.

## Server (API route)

```ts
import { streamObject } from 'ai';
import { z } from 'zod';

export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const { partialObject } = streamObject({
    model: 'gpt-4o',
    schema: z.object({
      title: z.string(),
      ingredients: z.array(z.string()),
      steps: z.array(z.string()),
    }),
    prompt: 'Give me a recipe for pancakes',
  });

  return partialObject.toTextStreamResponse();
}
```

## Client (React)

```tsx
import { useObject } from '@ai/react';
import { z } from 'zod';

const schema = z.object({
  title: z.string(),
  ingredients: z.array(z.string()),
  steps: z.array(z.string()),
});

function RecipeForm() {
  const { object, partialObject, submit } = useObject({
    api: '/api/recipe',
    schema,
  });

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => submit('Give me a recipe for pancakes')}>
        Generate
      </button>

      {/* partialObject updates as fields arrive */}
      {partialObject?.title && <h1>{partialObject.title}</h1>}

      {partialObject?.ingredients && (
        <ul>
          {partialObject.ingredients.map((item, i) => (
            <li key={i}>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}

      {partialObject?.steps && (
        <ol>
          {partialObject.steps.map((step, i) => (
            <li key={i}>{step}</li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Key points

| Property | Type | When it's set |
|----------|------|---------------|
| `partialObject` | `Partial<T> \| undefined` | Updates on each chunk |
| `object` | `T \| undefined` | Resolves when the stream completes |

- The schema (Zod) is what makes this "structured" — the SDK parses the streamed JSON incrementally and only hands you fields once they're fully valid.
- If you don't need the React hook, you can consume `partialObject.text` (a `ReadableStream<string>`) manually with `for await` or a `TextDecoder` loop.

## `result.usage` covers only the **last** step

When `generateText` runs a multi-step tool loop (via `maxSteps`), the returned `result.usage` reflects only the final LLM call. There is no built-in aggregated total.

## Getting per-step usage: `onStepFinish`

Use the `onStepFinish` callback to capture usage from every step:

```ts
import { generateText } from 'ai';

const stepUsages: Array<{ inputTokens: number; outputTokens: number }> = [];

const result = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-4o'),
  prompt: 'Plan a trip to Tokyo',
  tools: { getWeather, getFlights },
  maxSteps: 5,
  onStepFinish: ({ usage }) => {
    stepUsages.push({
      inputTokens: usage.promptTokens,
      outputTokens: usage.completionTokens,
    });
  },
});

// Last step only (same as result.usage)
console.log(result.usage);

// All steps
console.log(stepUsages);

// Total across all steps
const total = stepUsages.reduce(
  (acc, u) => ({
    inputTokens: acc.inputTokens + u.inputTokens,
    outputTokens: acc.outputTokens + u.outputTokens,
  }),
  { inputTokens: 0, outputTokens: 0 },
);
```

**TL;DR** — `result.usage` = last step. Accumulate via `onStepFinish` for the full picture.

## Using `maxSteps` (simplest)

When you set `maxSteps`, the SDK handles the tool-call loop internally and returns the full message history in the result:

```typescript
import { generateText } from "ai";
import { openai } from "@ai-sdk/openai";

const result = await generateText({
  model: openai("gpt-4o"),
  messages: [
    { role: "user", content: "What's the weather in Paris and Tokyo?" },
  ],
  tools: { getWeather },
  maxSteps: 5,
});

// result.messages contains the FULL conversation:
// [user, assistant (tool-call), tool (result), assistant (tool-call), tool (result), assistant (final text)]
await db.messages.save(result.messages);
```

## Manual loop (more control)

If you need to intercept each step (e.g., for streaming UI updates or conditional logic), you manage the array yourself:

```typescript
import { generateText, type CoreMessage } from "ai";
import { openai } from "@ai-sdk/openai";

let messages: CoreMessage[] = [
  { role: "user", content: "What's the weather in Paris?" },
];

while (true) {
  const result = await generateText({
    model: openai("gpt-4o"),
    messages,
    tools: { getWeather },
  });

  // Append the assistant's response (text + tool calls)
  messages.push({
    role: "assistant",
    content: [
      ...(result.text ? [{ type: "text", text: result.text }] : []),
      ...result.toolCalls.map((tc) => ({
        type: "tool-call",
        toolCallId: tc.toolCallId,
        toolName: tc.toolName,
        args: tc.args,
      })),
    ],
  });

  // No more tool calls → done
  if (result.toolCalls.length === 0) break;

  // Execute each tool and append results
  for (const tc of result.toolCalls) {
    const output = await executeTool(tc);
    messages.push({
      role: "tool",
      content: [
        {
          type: "tool-result",
          toolCallId: tc.toolCallId,
          toolName: tc.toolName,
          output,
        },
      ],
    });
  }
}

// `messages` is your full history — save it
await db.messages.save(messages);
```

## Key points

| | `maxSteps` | Manual loop |
|---|---|---|
| Full history | `result.messages` | Your `messages` variable |
| Per-step hooks | ❌ | ✅ |
| Simpler code | ✅ | ❌ |

The `CoreMessage[]` type is JSON-serializable, so you can store it directly in a JSON column or serialize it for a chat UI.

Use the `fullStream` property on the result of `streamText`. It's an async iterable that yields every event in order:

```ts
import { streamText, tool } from "ai";
import { z } from "zod";

const result = streamText({
  model: "gpt-4o",
  prompt: "What's the weather in Paris?",
  tools: {
    getWeather: tool({
      description: "Get current weather",
      parameters: z.object({ city: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ city }) => `Sunny, 22°C in ${city}`,
    }),
  },
});

for await (const event of result.fullStream) {
  switch (event.type) {
    case "text-delta":
      process.stdout.write(event.text);
      break;
    case "tool-call":
      console.log(`\n[tool-call] ${event.toolName}(${JSON.stringify(event.args)})`);
      break;
    case "tool-result":
      console.log(`[tool-result] ${event.toolName}: ${event.result}`);
      break;
    case "finish":
      console.log(`\n[finish] reason=${event.finishReason}`);
      break;
    case "error":
      console.error("[error]", event.error);
      break;
  }
}
```

**Available event types on `fullStream`:**

| `event.type` | Key fields |
|---|---|
| `start` | — |
| `start-step` | — |
| `text-delta` | `text` |
| `tool-call` | `toolCallId`, `toolName`, `args` |
| `tool-result` | `toolCallId`, `toolName`, `result` |
| `finish-step` | — |
| `finish` | `finishReason`, `usage` |
| `error` | `error` |

**Note:** `fullStream` is the low-level "everything" stream. If you only need text, `result.textStream` is simpler. If you only need the final result, `await result.text` / `await result.toolCalls` works too.

## Streaming UI Messages with `streamUI`

Use `streamUI` in your API route and return the result via `toDataStreamResponse()`. `useChat` on the client automatically parses the data stream.

### API Route (Next.js App Router)

```ts
// app/api/chat/route.ts
import { streamUI } from "ai";
import { openai } from "@ai-sdk/openai";

export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const { messages } = await req.json();

  const result = streamUI({
    model: openai("gpt-4o"),
    messages,
    generate: async ({ writer }) => {
      // 1. Send a UI message (rendered as a React component on the client)
      writer.write({
        id: "loading",
        role: "assistant",
        parts: [
          {
            type: "data",
            data: { type: "status", message: "Thinking..." },
          },
        ],
      });

      // 2. Merge in the text stream from the model
      const textStream = await writer.merge(
        // You can also call the model directly here if needed
        // e.g. await openai("gpt-4o").generate({ messages })
        // For now, the model output is already being streamed
      );

      // 3. Send another UI message after generation
      writer.write({
        id: "done",
        role: "assistant",
        parts: [
          {
            type: "data",
            data: { type: "status", message: "Done" },
          },
        ],
      });
    },
  });

  return result.toDataStreamResponse();
}
```

### Client

```tsx
"use client";
import { useChat } from "ai";

export default function Chat() {
  const { messages, input, handleInputChange, handleSubmit } = useChat({
    api: "/api/chat",
  });

  return (
    <div>
      {messages.map((m) => (
        <div key={m.id}>
          <strong>{m.role}:</strong>
          {m.parts.map((part) => {
            if (part.type === "text") return <p key={part.id}>{part.text}</p>;
            if (part.type === "data") {
              // Render your custom UI based on data
              return <StatusBadge key={part.id} data={part.data} />;
            }
            return null;
          })}
        </div>
      ))}
      <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
        <input
          value={input}
          onChange={handleInputChange}
        />
      </form>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Key points

| Concern | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Response format** | `toDataStreamResponse()` sets `Content-Type: text/event-stream` and the correct `x-vercel-ai-ui-message-stream` header. |
| **`writer.write()`** | Emits a full UI message (with `id`, `role`, `parts`). The client appends it to the message list. |
| **`writer.merge()`** | Merges a `CoreMessage[]` stream (e.g. from a model call) into the UI stream. |
| **`writer.writeData()`** | Sends a data-only chunk (no full message) — useful for progress updates. |
| **Client side** | `useChat` handles the SSE parsing, message accumulation, and part rendering automatically. No manual `EventSource` needed. |

If you only need plain text streaming (no interleaved UI messages), `streamText` + `toDataStreamResponse()` is simpler and `useChat` still works the same way.

## Requiring User Approval for a Tool

The AI SDK doesn't have a built-in `requiresApproval: true` flag on tools. The standard pattern is to make the tool's `execute` function **await user approval** before performing the destructive action.

### Pattern: Async approval gate inside `execute`

```typescript
import { tool } from "ai";
import { z } from "zod";
import fs from "fs/promises";

// A helper that returns a Promise resolved by your UI layer
// (WebSocket, SSE, HTTP callback, etc.)
function requestUserApproval(message: string): Promise<boolean> {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    // e.g., send to client via WebSocket
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: "approval_request", message }));

    // Listen for the user's response
    const handler = (data: string) => {
      const { approved } = JSON.parse(data);
      ws.off("message", handler);
      resolve(approved);
    };
    ws.on("message", handler);
  });
}

const deleteFile = tool({
  description: "Delete a file from the filesystem",
  parameters: z.object({
    path: z.string().describe("Absolute path of the file to delete"),
  }),
  execute: async ({ path }) => {
    const approved = await requestUserApproval(
      `⚠️ Delete "${path}"? This cannot be undone.`
    );

    if (!approved) {
      return { error: "User denied the operation." };
    }

    await fs.unlink(path);
    return { success: true, message: `Deleted ${path}` };
  },
});
```

### How it works in the flow

```
Model calls deleteFile → execute() runs → awaits approval
                                         ↓
                              UI shows confirmation dialog
                                         ↓
                              User clicks "Yes" / "No"
                                         ↓
                              Promise resolves → tool continues or returns error
```

The model sees the tool result (`{ error: ... }` or `{ success: ... }`) and can respond accordingly.

### Tips

- **Timeout** – add a timeout to `requestUserApproval` so a stale session doesn't hang the tool call forever.
- **Idempotency** – if the user approves but the operation fails, the model will see the error and can retry or inform the user.
- **Multiple tools** – if you have several destructive tools, you can extract the approval logic into a wrapper:

```typescript
function withApproval<T extends z.ZodTypeAny>(
  toolDef: Parameters<typeof tool>[0],
  approvalMessage: (args: z.infer<T>) => string
) {
  return tool({
    ...toolDef,
    execute: async (args: any) => {
      const approved = await requestUserApproval(approvalMessage(args));
      if (!approved) return { error: "User denied." };
      return toolDef.execute!(args);
    },
  });
}
```

This keeps the approval concern separate from the tool's actual logic.

Use a **factory function** (closure) so the `execute` function captures the key from its enclosing scope:

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

function createSearchTool(apiKey: string) {
  return {
    description: "Search the web",
    parameters: z.object({
      query: z.string().describe("Search query"),
    }),
    execute: async ({ query }) => {
      // `apiKey` is captured from the closure
      const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/search", {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${apiKey}` },
        body: JSON.stringify({ query }),
      });
      return res.json();
    },
  };
}

// At call time, inject the key from your environment
const tools = {
  search: createSearchTool(process.env.SEARCH_API_KEY!),
};

const result = await streamText({
  model: "openai/gpt-4o",
  tools,
  messages,
});
```

**Why not put the key in `parameters`?**

You *could* add it to the Zod schema, but that would expose the secret to the model (it appears in the prompt and in the tool-call JSON sent to the LLM provider). Keep secrets out of the model's context.

**Alternative: pass it via a shared context object**

If you have many tools that need the same credentials, build a small context:

```ts
type ToolContext = {
  searchApiKey: string;
  dbUrl: string;
};

function createTools(ctx: ToolContext) {
  return {
    search: {
      description: "Search the web",
      parameters: z.object({ query: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ query }) => {
        // uses ctx.searchApiKey
      },
    },
    queryDb: {
      description: "Query the database",
      parameters: z.object({ sql: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ sql }) => {
        // uses ctx.dbUrl
      },
    },
  };
}
```

This keeps tool definitions pure and makes them easy to test with mock credentials.

# OpenTelemetry Tracing in the AI SDK

The AI SDK has built-in OpenTelemetry support. You need to:

1. Install the OpenTelemetry packages
2. Register a `TracerProvider`
3. Enable telemetry (globally or per-call)

## Setup

```bash
npm install @opentelemetry/api @opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node @opentelemetry/otlp-exporter-node
```

## Global Telemetry

```ts
// otel.ts — import this early in your app (e.g., in your server entry point)
import { NodeTracerProvider } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node';
import { BatchSpanProcessor } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-node';
import { OTLPTraceExporter } from '@opentelemetry/otlp-exporter-node';
import { setGlobalTelemetry } from 'ai';

const provider = new NodeTracerProvider({
  spanProcessors: [
    new BatchSpanProcessor(
      new OTLPTraceExporter({
        url: 'http://localhost:4318/v1/traces', // e.g., Jaeger, Grafana Tempo
      })
    ),
  ],
});

provider.register();

// Enable telemetry for ALL AI SDK calls
setGlobalTelemetry({
  isEnabled: true,
  functionId: 'my-app',
  metadata: {
    // any custom metadata attached to every span
    environment: 'production',
  },
});
```

## Per-Call Telemetry (override or use without global)

```ts
import { generateText } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

const { text } = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-4o'),
  prompt: 'Hello',
  experimental_telemetry: {
    isEnabled: true,
    functionId: 'chat-completion',
    metadata: { userId: 'user-123' },
  },
});
```

## What Gets Traced

Each AI SDK call produces spans for:

- The top-level call (e.g., `generateText`)
- The LLM provider request
- Token usage (input/output tokens) as span attributes

## Notes

- `setGlobalTelemetry` must be called **after** `provider.register()` so the global tracer is available.
- The `experimental_` prefix on the per-call option reflects that the telemetry API is still stabilizing.
- If you're using a framework like Next.js, put the `otel.ts` import at the top of your `instrumentation.ts` or server entry file.

If you're on a very recent version and `setGlobalTelemetry` isn't exported, check the `ai` package's exports — the name may have shifted as the API moves out of experimental.

## Reading Prompt Cache Tokens

The `usage` object on the result includes provider-specific cache fields. For **Anthropic** (which has explicit prompt caching):

```typescript
import { generateText } from 'ai';
import { anthropic } from '@ai-sdk/anthropic';

const result = await generateText({
  model: anthropic('claude-sonnet-4-20250514'),
  prompt: '...',
});

console.log(result.usage.cacheReadInputTokens);      // tokens served from cache
console.log(result.usage.cacheCreationInputTokens);  // tokens written to cache
console.log(result.usage.promptTokens);              // total prompt tokens
```

For **OpenAI** (automatic prompt caching), the cached portion is reported as:

```typescript
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

const result = await generateText({
  model: openai('gpt-4o'),
  prompt: '...',
});

// OpenAI reports cached tokens as a subset of promptTokens
// The field is `cachedTokens` on the usage object
console.log(result.usage.cachedTokens);
```

### Notes

- These fields are **provider-specific** — they only appear when the underlying provider supports and reports prompt caching.
- `cacheReadInputTokens` + `cacheCreationInputTokens` + non-cached tokens ≈ `promptTokens` (for Anthropic).
- If you're using `streamText`, the same `usage` object is available on the final `onFinish` callback or the `fullStream` result.

If you're on a version where these fields aren't typed, you can cast:

```typescript
const usage = result.usage as any;
console.log(usage.cacheReadInputTokens);
```

I'm most confident about the Anthropic field names. If you're targeting a specific provider and the field isn't showing up, check the provider's raw API docs for the exact usage field name — the SDK passes it through in camelCase.

```ts
import { createOpenAI } from "@ai-sdk/openai";
import { embeddings } from "ai";

const openai = createOpenAI({ apiKey: "sk-..." });

const { embedding } = await embeddings({
  model: openai.embedding("text-embedding-3-small"),
  value: "Hello world",
});

console.log(embedding); // number[]
```

- `openai.embedding("text-embedding-3-small")` returns a model reference (no API call yet).
- `embeddings()` performs the actual call and returns `{ embedding: number[], usage: { tokens: number } }`.
- `value` can also be an array of strings to batch-embed.

## Connecting to a Remote MCP Server with `streamText`

Use the `@ai-sdk/mcp` package's `createMCPClient` together with a transport from `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`:

```typescript
import { streamText } from 'ai';
import { createMCPClient } from '@ai-sdk/mcp';
import { StreamableHTTPClientTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/streamableHttp.js';

// 1. Create a transport to your remote MCP server
const transport = new StreamableHTTPClientTransport(
  new URL('http://your-mcp-server:3000/mcp'),
);

// 2. Create the MCP client
const client = await createMCPClient({ transport });

// 3. Get AI-SDK-compatible tools from the server
const tools = await client.tools();

// 4. Use them with streamText
const { fullText } = await streamText({
  model: 'gpt-4o',
  prompt: 'What files are in the project?',
  tools,
  maxSteps: 5, // allow the model to call tools multiple times
});

console.log(await fullText);
```

### Key points

| Concern | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Transport** | Use `StreamableHTTPClientTransport` for the newer Streamable HTTP spec, or `SSEClientTransport` for older SSE-based servers. |
| **`client.tools()`** | Returns a plain object of AI-SDK tool definitions (with `description` and `execute`), ready to pass to `streamText` / `generateText`. |
| **`maxSteps`** | Required (or set > 1) so the model can loop: call tool → get result → call another tool → … |
| **Cleanup** | Call `await client.close()` when you're done to terminate the connection. |

### SSE-based servers (older spec)

```typescript
import { SSEClientTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/client/sse.js';

const transport = new SSEClientTransport(new URL('http://your-mcp-server:3000/sse'));
const client = await createMCPClient({ transport });
const tools = await client.tools();
```

> **Note:** If your MCP server requires auth, pass headers via the transport constructor's options (e.g. `{ requestInit: { headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer …' } } }`).

**No.** The Vercel AI SDK does **not** automatically prompt the user for approval when an MCP tool is annotated with `destructiveHint: true`.

The `destructiveHint` is a metadata annotation defined by the MCP spec. The SDK passes it through as part of the tool's metadata, but it does not intercept execution or add any approval gate.

### What you need to do yourself

You can wrap the tool's `execute` function to add your own approval logic:

```ts
import { createMCPClient } from "@ai-sdk/mcp";

const client = await createMCPClient({
  transport: { type: "stdio", command: ["node", "my-mcp-server.js"] },
});

const tools = await client.tools();

// Wrap destructive tools with an approval step
const safeTools = Object.fromEntries(
  Object.entries(tools).map(([name, tool]) => {
    if (tool.annotations?.destructiveHint) {
      return [
        name,
        {
          ...tool,
          execute: async (args: any, options: any) => {
            const approved = await askUserForApproval(
              `Tool "${name}" is marked as destructive. Proceed?`,
            );
            if (!approved) {
              throw new Error("User denied execution.");
            }
            return tool.execute(args, options);
          },
        },
      ];
    }
    return [name, tool];
  }),
);
```

### Key points

| Aspect | Behavior |
|--------|----------|
| `destructiveHint` visibility | The model *can* see it in the tool metadata, so it may be more cautious, but it's not a hard gate. |
| Automatic approval | ❌ Not implemented in the SDK. |
| Your responsibility | Implement the approval flow (UI prompt, webhook, etc.) in your application layer. |

The SDK's role is to bridge MCP tools into the `generateText` / `streamText` tool-calling loop. Policy decisions like "ask before running destructive operations" are intentionally left to the application.

## Short answer: No, there's no built-in auto-retry for MCP tool calls.

The `maxRetries` option on `generateText`/`streamText` retries the **LLM API call** (e.g., a 429 from OpenAI), not individual tool executions. When an MCP tool call fails, the SDK catches the error and returns it as a tool result to the model — the model then decides whether to retry or handle the error.

## Practical solution: wrap your tool handler with retry logic

```ts
import { createMCPClient } from "@ai-sdk/mcp";

const mcp = await createMCPClient({
  transport: {
    type: "stdio",
    command: "npx",
    args: ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "/tmp"],
  },
});

// Retry wrapper
function withRetry<T>(fn: () => Promise<T>, opts?: {
  maxAttempts?: number;
  baseDelayMs?: number;
}): Promise<T> {
  const maxAttempts = opts?.maxAttempts ?? 3;
  const baseDelay = opts?.baseDelayMs ?? 1000;

  return (async () => {
    for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= maxAttempts; attempt++) {
      try {
        return await fn();
      } catch (err) {
        if (attempt === maxAttempts) throw err;

        // Only retry on transient errors
        const msg = String(err);
        const isTransient =
          msg.includes("429") ||
          msg.includes("502") ||
          msg.includes("503") ||
          msg.includes("504") ||
          msg.includes("timeout");

        if (!isTransient) throw err;

        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, baseDelay * 2 ** (attempt - 1)));
      }
    }
    throw new Error("unreachable");
  })();
}

// Use it when calling tools manually
const result = await withRetry(() =>
  mcp.callTool({ name: "read_file", args: { path: "/tmp/data.json" } })
);
```

## If tools are used inside `generateText` (agent loop)

When the model calls tools via the `tools` option, you can wrap each tool's `execute` function:

```ts
import { generateText } from "ai";

const { tools } = await mcp.tools();

// Wrap each tool with retry
const retryTools = Object.fromEntries(
  Object.entries(tools).map(([name, tool]) => [
    name,
    {
      ...tool,
      execute: async (args: any, ctx) =>
        withRetry(() => tool.execute!(args, ctx), { maxAttempts: 3 }),
    },
  ])
);

const { text } = await generateText({
  model: "gpt-4o",
  tools: retryTools,
  maxSteps: 5, // let the model retry on its own too
  prompt: "Read /tmp/data.json and summarize it",
});
```

## Key points

| Layer | Retries? | How |
|-------|----------|-----|
| LLM API call (429/500 from provider) | ✅ Built-in | `maxRetries` option |
| MCP tool execution | ❌ No | You must wrap `execute` |
| Model deciding to re-call a tool | ✅ Indirect | `maxSteps` + error returned as tool result |

The `maxSteps` option is useful here: if a tool returns an error, the model sees it and can naturally retry the call on the next step — but that's model-driven, not deterministic. For reliability, the explicit wrapper is the way to go.

## You have two options

### 1. Client-side with `onFinish` (most common)

The `messages` array from `useChat` already includes the completed AI message by the time `onFinish` fires:

```typescript
'use client';
import { useChat } from 'ai/react';

function Chat() {
  const { messages, input, handleInputChange, handleSubmit } = useChat({
    onFinish: async () => {
      // `messages` now includes the final AI message
      await fetch('/api/save-conversation', {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify({ messages }),
      });
    },
  });

  // ...
}
```

### 2. Server-side in the API route

`streamText` exposes a `.text` promise that resolves to the full response once the stream completes. You can fire-and-forget a save:

```typescript
// app/api/chat/route.ts
import { streamText } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';

export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const { messages, userId } = await req.json();

  const result = streamText({
    model: openai('gpt-4o'),
    messages,
  });

  // Save in the background once the full text is available
  result.text.then(async (text) => {
    const fullConversation = [
      ...messages,
      { role: 'assistant' as const, content: text },
    ];
    await db.conversations.create({
      data: { userId, messages: fullConversation },
    });
  }).catch(console.error);

  return result.toTextStreamResponse();
}
```

> **Note:** The `result.text` promise resolves after the model finishes generating, but the response stream to the client may still be in flight. That's fine — the save is independent.

### Which to pick?

| | Client (`onFinish`) | Server (API route) |
|---|---|---|
| Has access to | Full `messages` array as the user sees it | `messages` sent by client + generated text |
| Survives client disconnect? | ❌ | ✅ |
| Simpler | ✅ | Slightly more code |

If you need durability (e.g., the user closes the tab mid-stream), go with the **server-side** approach. Otherwise `onFinish` is the least friction.

